---
description: Fretwise product constraints, stack, and code style
alwaysApply: true
---

This project is Fretwise, a browser guitar coach for adult beginners who cannot
read music. Non-negotiable constraints:

- NEVER render standard notation or tablature staves. Everything is visual:
  chord diagrams, moving beat indicators, colour, animation.
- The user knows open chords but struggles with strumming and chord changes.
  Never condescend. No streaks, no badges, no guilt.
- Feedback is descriptive and specific, never evaluative. "You're landing about
  40ms early" not "Wrong" or "Score: 62%". No red X marks. No failure states.
  Never emit generic encouragement ("great job", "keep it up"). Every statement
  must cite a measured number.
- Audio timing must be sample-accurate via AudioContext scheduling.
  NEVER use setInterval or setTimeout for musical timing.
- All DSP runs in AudioWorklets, never on the main thread.
- Every exercise has a tempo control that goes down to 40 BPM.
- Mic audio is processed locally. Audio is never uploaded without explicit
  per-recording opt-in.

Stack: Vite + React + TypeScript, Tailwind, Zustand, Vitest, React Router.
Libraries: pitchy, svguitar, tonal, dexie, chordsheetjs.
Deployment: Vercel, static SPA, output dir dist.

Code style: DSP as small pure functions, unit tested in isolation against
fixture arrays — never tested by ear. Comment the maths in plain English.
